From everything we've built together—from the Go banking microservices to your current Rust studies—you're now at the stage where the remaining improvements are about security hardening and production polish, not basic implementation.

The next thing I'd recommend learning is authentication security: refresh-token families, reuse detection strategies, token versioning, device/session management, and how large systems like Google or GitHub handle login sessions. Those concepts will take your authentication system from "works well" to "production-grade."

What I recommend next

Before moving to the Account Service, I'd like us to make your authentication fully production-ready by implementing these security features:

Refresh token reuse attack detection (revoke the entire token family if an old refresh token is reused).
JWT jti (unique token ID) support.
Token family IDs for tracking refresh token chains.
Device/session management (list active sessions and revoke a specific session).
Optional Redis-backed JWT blacklist for immediate access-token invalidation after logout.

These are advanced features used by banking systems and large-scale applications. Since you're building EucastanPay, I think it's worth implementing them before moving on.
